# UniFi Network backup recovery

This directory contains encrypted UniFi Network application backups.
Filenames follow `<prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-network-<version>.unf.age`, where
`<version>` is the Network application version that made the backup. Each
file is an age-encrypted `.unf`, the Network application's own backup
format.

## What a .unf holds, and what it doesn't

A `.unf` holds the **Network application** only: networks, Wi-Fi, firewall,
routing, adopted devices, clients and their settings. It does **not** hold
UniFi OS itself: local admin accounts, Control Plane settings and other
applications. Those are only in a **System Config Backup** (`.unifi`), made
in Settings > Control Plane > Backups (Super Admin or owner only). After a
`.unf` restore, recreate what UniFi OS lost by hand, or restore a `.unifi`
first.

A `.unf` restores only onto the **same or a newer** Network application
version than the one in its filename.

## To decrypt one file

You need an age identity (private key) whose public key was listed in
`AGE_RECIPIENTS` at backup time.

```sh
age -d -i /path/to/your.key -o backup.unf <prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-network-<version>.unf.age
```

The `.unf` contains Wi-Fi passwords, admin password hashes and VPN keys.
Keep it on an encrypted volume only and shred it when you're done.

### If the recipient was a YubiKey

If the backup was encrypted to a YubiKey recipient (`age1yubikey1...`), you
need both [`age`](https://age-encryption.org/) and
[`age-plugin-yubikey`](https://github.com/str4d/age-plugin-yubikey) installed,
a running PC/SC daemon (`pcscd`), and one of the YubiKeys that was listed as a
recipient inserted.

```sh
# With the YubiKey inserted, export a stub identity for it:
age-plugin-yubikey --identity > yubikey-identity.txt

# Decrypt (you will be prompted for the PIN and a touch):
age -d -i yubikey-identity.txt -o backup.unf <prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-network-<version>.unf.age

# The identity stub holds no secret (just a pointer to the hardware slot):
rm yubikey-identity.txt
```

Any one of the YubiKeys listed at backup time works — you do not need all of
them.

## To restore

1. Bring the gateway or console up, set it up and update the Network
   application to at least the version in the filename. Leave the other
   UniFi devices connected as they were.
2. Settings > Control Plane > Backups > Restore, then upload `backup.unf`.
3. Wait for every device to come back online.

Ubiquiti's guide: <https://help.ui.com/hc/en-us/articles/360008976393>
("Backups and Migration in UniFi", section "Network-Only Backups").
